BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Accumulated misstatements approaching performance materiality
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Ridgeline Electronics, the auditor has accumulated identified misstatements totaling $42,000 against a performance materiality of $50,000. No additional audit procedures have been performed in the remaining untested areas. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Consider whether the overall audit strategy and plan need revision", "Issue a qualified opinion based on the accumulated misstatements", "Accept the misstatements as immaterial since they are below performance materiality", "Request management to adjust all identified misstatements before continuing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A13, when accumulated misstatements approach performance materiality, the auditor should consider whether the overall audit strategy needs revision, including potentially expanding procedures. The misstatements have not yet exceeded materiality, so a qualified opinion is premature. Unlike simply accepting misstatements below the threshold, the auditor must evaluate whether undetected misstatements combined with known ones could exceed materiality.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L4 — Iron curtain vs. rollover approach for evaluating misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Pinnacle Financial Group has a $30,000 revenue overstatement originating in the prior year that reversed in the current year. The current year also has a new $25,000 expense understatement. Overall materiality is $100,000. When evaluating whether the financial statements are materially misstated, the auditor should consider:', '["Only the current-year $25,000 understatement since the prior-year error self-corrected", "The $55,000 combined total of both misstatements regardless of their periods", "Both the cumulative balance sheet effect and the current-year income statement effect of each misstatement", "Only whether each individual misstatement exceeds materiality on its own"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.11, the auditor evaluates the aggregate effect of uncorrected misstatements, considering both the cumulative balance sheet effect and the current-year income statement impact. Simply netting self-correcting errors ignores the balance sheet impact. Unlike evaluating only the combined total, the auditor must assess each period''s financial statements separately to determine whether users would be misled by either the balance sheet or income statement presentation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Management bias in estimates indicating fraud risk
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Cascade Industries, the auditor observes that management consistently selects assumptions at the aggressive end of reasonable ranges for the allowance for loan losses, warranty reserve, and pension obligations. Each estimate individually falls within an acceptable range. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Accept each estimate since they fall within their respective acceptable ranges", "Evaluate whether the pattern of bias, taken collectively, indicates a risk of material misstatement due to fraud", "Require management to adjust all three estimates to the midpoint of their ranges", "Disclose the aggressive estimates as an emphasis-of-matter paragraph in the audit report"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A20, when uncorrected misstatements share a common characteristic suggesting directional bias, the auditor evaluates them collectively and considers whether the cumulative effect indicates a material misstatement, including potential fraud risk. Individual reasonableness does not preclude collective concern. Unlike requiring midpoint adjustments, the standard asks auditors to assess the collective bias pattern rather than mandate specific estimate values.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Design deficiency vs. operating deficiency scenario
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Harvest Medical Supplies, the auditor finds that the company''s accounts payable system has an automated three-way match for invoice approval. However, the system was configured to allow overrides without secondary authorization. The auditor should classify this as:', '["An operating deficiency because the control exists but failed to operate effectively", "A material weakness because the override capability creates significant financial risk", "A significant deficiency because management could exploit the override function", "A design deficiency because the control was not properly designed to prevent unauthorized overrides"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A6, a design deficiency exists when a control necessary to meet a control objective is missing or not properly designed to achieve its purpose, even if operated as designed. The override without secondary authorization represents a flaw in the control''s design. Unlike an operating deficiency, where a properly designed control fails to operate effectively, this deficiency exists in how the control was configured from inception.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Combined effect of individually immaterial deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Meridian Hospitality Group, the auditor identifies three control deficiencies: (1) no independent review of journal entries below $10,000, (2) lack of segregation between cash receipts and bank deposits, and (3) no reconciliation of credit card settlement reports to the general ledger. Individually, each deficiency is assessed as a significant deficiency. The auditor should next:', '["Report all three as significant deficiencies since none individually qualifies as a material weakness", "Evaluate whether any single deficiency could reasonably result in a material misstatement to the financial statements", "Communicate each deficiency separately to management without further aggregation analysis", "Assess whether the combination of deficiencies, considered together, constitutes a material weakness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, the auditor considers whether individual control deficiencies that are not material weaknesses may, in combination, represent a material weakness. All three deficiencies affect the completeness and accuracy of cash and revenue, creating overlapping exposure. Unlike reporting them separately as significant deficiencies, the auditor must evaluate the aggregate effect on the likelihood and magnitude of potential misstatements before finalizing the classification.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Reassessing materiality upon discovering misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Clearwater Construction, the auditor initially set overall materiality at $200,000. Midway through fieldwork, the auditor identifies misstatements totaling $175,000 and discovers that the company''s pretax income is significantly lower than originally projected. Under AU-C 320, the auditor should:', '["Continue with the original materiality level since it was set during planning", "Automatically issue a qualified opinion because misstatements approach materiality", "Revise materiality downward and evaluate whether additional audit procedures are needed", "Increase the sample sizes for remaining procedures but maintain the original materiality"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 320.12, the auditor revises materiality if information obtained during the audit indicates that a lower amount would have been appropriate at planning. The lower pretax income changes the benchmark basis. Unlike maintaining the original materiality, the auditor must reconsider both the level and whether previously sufficient procedures remain adequate under the revised threshold. A qualified opinion is premature without completing the reassessment.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — IT general control weakness as material weakness
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Atlas Digital Solutions, the auditor discovers that the company''s ERP system allows any user with standard access to modify financial master data, including vendor records, chart of accounts, and employee pay rates, without logging or supervisory approval. The ERP processes approximately $50 million in annual transactions. The auditor should most likely classify this finding as:', '["A significant deficiency because no actual misstatement has been detected from the access issue", "A design deficiency that requires additional testing before severity can be assessed", "An operating deficiency because the logging feature exists but was not activated", "A material weakness because unrestricted access to master data creates a reasonable possibility of material misstatement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, a material weakness exists when there is a reasonable possibility that a material misstatement will not be prevented or detected on a timely basis. Unrestricted modification of financial master data across $50 million in transactions represents both high magnitude and high likelihood of undetected misstatement. Unlike classifying this as merely a significant deficiency, the absence of both preventive and detective controls over critical data elevates severity regardless of whether an actual misstatement has occurred.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Definition of "clearly trivial" misstatement threshold
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what is the significance of the "clearly trivial" threshold for identified misstatements?', '["Misstatements below this threshold need not be accumulated by the auditor", "Misstatements below this threshold must still be communicated to management", "Misstatements below this threshold are automatically considered immaterial", "Misstatements below this threshold require documentation but no further evaluation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.05, the auditor accumulates identified misstatements during the audit except those that are clearly trivial. "Clearly trivial" is not the same as "not material" — it represents a much lower threshold below which misstatements are inconsequential individually and in aggregate. Unlike the immateriality threshold, clearly trivial means the items are too small to warrant any further consideration regardless of aggregation effects.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Three categories of misstatements under AU-C 450
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what are the three categories of misstatements that an auditor should distinguish when evaluating identified errors?', '["Material, immaterial, and clearly trivial misstatements", "Factual, judgmental, and projected misstatements", "Quantitative, qualitative, and pervasive misstatements", "Known, likely, and possible misstatements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 450.A3 identifies three categories: factual misstatements (errors about which there is no doubt), judgmental misstatements (differences arising from management''s judgments the auditor considers unreasonable), and projected misstatements (the auditor''s best estimate of misstatements in populations based on sample results). Unlike "material, immaterial, and clearly trivial," which describe severity levels, these three categories describe the nature and source of the identified misstatement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Definition of tolerable misstatement in sampling
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'In the context of audit sampling, what is the meaning of "tolerable misstatement" as defined in AU-C 530?', '["The maximum misstatement that the auditor considers clearly trivial", "The total amount of uncorrected misstatements carried from prior periods", "The application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure", "The threshold above which a qualified opinion must be issued"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 530.05, tolerable misstatement is the application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure. It represents the maximum misstatement in a population that the auditor is willing to accept and still conclude the audit objective is achieved. Unlike overall materiality, tolerable misstatement is set at the individual test level and is typically lower than financial statement materiality to allow for undetected misstatements across all areas.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Qualitative factors making a quantitatively small misstatement material
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Northshore Manufacturing, the auditor identifies a $15,000 uncorrected misstatement in revenue recognition. Overall materiality is $500,000. However, the misstatement changes the company''s current ratio from 2.01 to 1.98, which breaches a bank loan covenant requiring a minimum ratio of 2.0. The auditor should:', '["Conclude the misstatement is immaterial because $15,000 is well below the $500,000 materiality level", "Issue a qualified opinion solely because the misstatement exceeds the clearly trivial threshold", "Require management to correct the misstatement before evaluating the audit opinion", "Consider the misstatement material due to its qualitative effect on loan covenant compliance"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.A21, the auditor evaluates both quantitative and qualitative aspects of uncorrected misstatements. A misstatement that triggers a debt covenant breach has qualitative significance that may render it material regardless of its dollar amount relative to overall materiality. Unlike applying only a quantitative threshold, the auditor must assess whether the misstatement could influence decisions of financial statement users, including lenders monitoring covenant compliance.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — Pervasiveness of control deficiency across multiple accounts
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Grandview Manufacturing, the auditor discovers that the company lacks a formal process for reconciling subledgers to the general ledger for accounts receivable, inventory, and fixed assets. Each subledger contains material account balances. The auditor determines that this single deficiency affects multiple financial statement assertions across several line items. This finding is most significant because:', '["A deficiency affecting multiple material accounts increases the likelihood and magnitude of potential undetected misstatements", "The deficiency automatically qualifies as a material weakness since three or more accounts are involved", "Reconciliation controls are always classified as key controls regardless of account materiality", "The auditor must withdraw from the engagement when pervasive deficiencies are identified"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A10, the severity of a control deficiency depends on the magnitude and likelihood of potential misstatement. A deficiency affecting multiple material accounts increases both dimensions because errors in any of the affected subledgers could go undetected. Unlike applying an automatic threshold based on the number of affected accounts, the auditor exercises judgment about the combined exposure. The auditor does not withdraw but rather evaluates severity and communicates appropriately.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Compensating controls reducing deficiency severity
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Silverline Logistics, the auditor identifies that the warehouse manager has both custody of inventory and authority to record inventory adjustments without supervisory approval. However, the controller performs a detailed monthly inventory variance analysis comparing physical counts to recorded balances and investigates discrepancies exceeding $2,000. The auditor should:', '["Classify the deficiency as a material weakness because segregation of duties is fundamentally compromised", "Ignore the deficiency because the compensating control fully eliminates the risk", "Report the segregation issue as a significant deficiency without considering the compensating control", "Evaluate whether the compensating control reduces the severity of the deficiency to below significant deficiency level"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A13, the auditor considers compensating controls when evaluating the severity of a control deficiency. The monthly variance analysis by the controller is a detective compensating control that may reduce the likelihood of material misstatement from the segregation weakness. Unlike ignoring the deficiency entirely, the auditor must evaluate whether the compensating control operates effectively and its precision is sufficient. Automatic classification as a material weakness disregards mitigating controls.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Timing of written communication of control deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, when must the auditor provide written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses to those charged with governance?', '["Before releasing the audit report to prevent delays in financial statement issuance", "No later than 60 days following the report release date", "Within 30 days after the end of the entity''s fiscal year", "Immediately upon identification during the course of fieldwork"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 265.13 requires the auditor to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. This timeline allows the auditor to complete the evaluation of all identified deficiencies before issuing the communication. Unlike immediate communication during fieldwork, the written communication deadline ensures the auditor has assessed the full scope of deficiencies before formalizing the report.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Known vs. projected misstatement in sampling
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Pacific Trading Company, the auditor selects a sample of 40 sales invoices from a population of 5,000 and finds two invoices with pricing errors totaling $3,200. The auditor extrapolates this to the population and estimates the total misstatement at $400,000, compared to a tolerable misstatement of $350,000. The $3,200 found in the sample is classified as:', '["A projected misstatement because it was found through sampling", "A likely misstatement that combines the known and sampling errors", "A known misstatement representing actual errors identified in the items tested", "A clearly trivial amount that does not require further evaluation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A3, the $3,200 represents actual errors with no ambiguity — factual misstatements found in specific items tested. These are known misstatements. The $400,000 extrapolation is the projected misstatement representing the auditor''s best estimate of the total population error. Unlike classifying sample findings as projected, projected misstatements arise from extending sample results to the untested population, not from the errors directly observed in tested items.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Re-communication of prior-year unremediated deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bayside Electronics, the auditor identifies that a significant deficiency in the payroll processing controls, first communicated in the prior year, has not been remediated by management. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Reference the prior-year communication without issuing a new written communication", "Omit the deficiency from the current-year communication since it was previously reported", "Automatically escalate the deficiency to a material weakness due to management''s inaction", "Re-communicate the significant deficiency in writing in the current year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A18, if a previously communicated significant deficiency remains unremediated, the auditor should re-communicate it in the current period. The deficiency still exists and represents ongoing risk; the prior communication does not substitute for current-year reporting. Unlike automatically escalating to a material weakness, failure to remediate does not by itself change the deficiency''s severity classification, though the auditor should reassess severity based on current conditions.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Purpose of the summary of uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the purpose of the summary of uncorrected misstatements that the auditor prepares under AU-C 450?', '["To document all identified misstatements that management has not corrected for evaluation and communication purposes", "To provide management with a list of adjusting journal entries required before the audit report is issued", "To calculate the exact amount by which the financial statements are misstated", "To serve as the auditor''s formal recommendation for restatement of prior-period financials"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.12, the auditor communicates all uncorrected misstatements to the appropriate level of management and requests correction. The summary documents these misstatements to support the auditor''s evaluation of whether uncorrected items, individually or in aggregate, are material. Unlike a list of required adjustments, the summary serves an evaluative and communication purpose — management may decline to correct items and the auditor then evaluates that conclusion.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Management representation letter regarding uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After completing the audit of Crestline Automotive, the auditor communicates five uncorrected misstatements totaling $45,000, well below the $300,000 materiality threshold. Management declines to correct them. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should obtain from management:', '["A revised set of financial statements that discloses the uncorrected misstatements in the footnotes", "A written representation that management believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial to the financial statements", "A verbal acknowledgment during the closing meeting that the misstatements were considered and rejected", "A formal waiver signed by the audit committee approving management''s refusal to correct"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor requests a written representation from management that it believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. A summary of the uncorrected items is included in or attached to the representation letter. Unlike a verbal acknowledgment, the written representation provides documented evidence of management''s assertion and is a required element of the audit file.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Disclosure misstatement evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakewood Pharmaceuticals, the auditor discovers that the company omitted a required footnote disclosing $8 million in pending product liability litigation, although the potential loss has been properly accrued. Materiality is $5 million. The auditor should evaluate this omission as:', '["Immaterial because the financial statements already reflect the accrued liability amount", "An insignificant disclosure deficiency that requires only a management letter comment", "A material misstatement because the omission of the required disclosure deprives users of relevant information", "A scope limitation that prevents the auditor from expressing an unmodified opinion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A4, misstatements include misstatements of disclosures. Even when amounts are properly recorded, the omission of a required disclosure is itself a misstatement that the auditor evaluates for materiality. An $8 million litigation matter exceeding the $5 million materiality threshold represents information users need for decision-making. Unlike treating this as a minor deficiency because the amount is accrued, the disclosure requirement exists independently to provide transparency about contingent exposures.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Required recipients of material weakness communications
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, which parties must receive the auditor''s written communication about identified material weaknesses?', '["The Securities and Exchange Commission and the entity''s external legal counsel", "The entity''s shareholders at the next annual meeting", "Only management, since material weaknesses are operational matters", "Those charged with governance and management"]'::jsonb, 3, 'AU-C 265.11 requires the auditor to communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to both those charged with governance and management. Management receives the communication because they are responsible for designing and implementing internal controls, while governance bodies need the information for oversight. Unlike communicating only to management, the standard ensures those charged with governance are informed to fulfill their oversight responsibilities over financial reporting.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Reassessing severity of unremediated deficiency with resulting misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While auditing Summit Retail Group, the auditor finds that a significant deficiency involving inadequate review of manual journal entries, reported for the past two consecutive years, remains unremediated. During the current year, the auditor identifies two misstatements totaling $120,000 directly attributable to unreviewed journal entries. Materiality is $250,000. The auditor should:', '["Maintain the classification as a significant deficiency since no single misstatement exceeded materiality", "Reassess whether the continued deficiency and resulting misstatements now constitute a material weakness", "Report the deficiency for a third time using the same classification and language as prior years", "Issue an adverse opinion because management has failed to address the deficiency after repeated notification"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, the auditor reassesses the severity of a deficiency each year based on current conditions. The combination of persistent non-remediation and actual misstatements attributable to the control gap may change the likelihood assessment, potentially elevating the deficiency to a material weakness. Unlike automatically maintaining the prior classification, the auditor must evaluate whether changed circumstances alter the risk profile. An adverse opinion requires pervasive material misstatement, not merely an unremediated deficiency.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Systematic cause of misstatements suggesting larger population error
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Woodbridge Distribution, the auditor identifies pricing errors in 6 of 30 sampled sales transactions, all resulting from the same system configuration issue in the automated pricing module. The total identified error is $18,000 against a tolerable misstatement of $75,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should give particular attention to:', '["Whether the systematic nature of the errors suggests the projected misstatement may be significantly larger than the sample indicates", "Whether the $18,000 known error alone exceeds the clearly trivial threshold", "Whether each of the six errors individually exceeds performance materiality", "Whether the system configuration can be corrected before the financial statements are issued"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A8, when misstatements share a common cause, the auditor considers that the systematic nature may indicate the population contains a proportionally larger number of similar errors. The error rate of 20% applied to the full population could yield misstatements well above tolerable levels. Unlike focusing solely on the known error amount, the auditor must evaluate whether the pattern suggests a pervasive population-level issue requiring expanded procedures or complete restatement of affected transactions.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Evaluating overstatements and understatements in same account
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Bridgeport Electronics, the auditor finds that inventory is overstated by $60,000 due to obsolete items and simultaneously understated by $45,000 due to an unrecorded purchase. When accumulating misstatements under AU-C 450, the auditor should report the effect on inventory as:', '["Only the $60,000 overstatement since it represents the more significant error", "A net $15,000 overstatement by offsetting the two misstatements", "Both misstatements separately, recognizing the $60,000 overstatement and the $45,000 understatement", "Only the $45,000 understatement because unrecorded transactions have higher audit risk"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A7, the auditor accumulates misstatements without automatically offsetting them, considering both the gross and net effects when evaluating their impact on the financial statements. Each misstatement has a different cause and affects different assertions — valuation versus completeness. Unlike netting the two errors, treating them separately preserves information about the nature and cause of each misstatement, which is essential for evaluating whether they indicate broader control issues.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Distinguishing design deficiency from operating deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Canyon Creek Services, the auditor observes that the company has a policy requiring dual authorization for payments exceeding $5,000. The auditor tests 25 such payments and finds that 4 were approved by only one person. Under AU-C 265, this finding is best classified as:', '["A material weakness because the exception rate exceeds 10% of tested items", "A design deficiency because the dual-authorization threshold should be lower", "A significant deficiency because management is aware of the policy but chose to override it", "An operating deficiency because a properly designed control did not operate as intended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A7, an operating deficiency exists when a properly designed control does not operate effectively or the person performing the control lacks the authority or competence to do so. The dual-authorization policy is a well-designed control, but it was not consistently executed. Unlike a design deficiency, where the control itself is missing or inadequate, this deficiency arises from a failure in execution of an otherwise appropriate control procedure.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Auditor's documentation of clearly trivial threshold
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what must the auditor document regarding the threshold used for accumulating misstatements?', '["Only the overall materiality amount established during audit planning", "The amount below which misstatements are considered clearly trivial", "A formal calculation showing how materiality was allocated to each account balance", "The total projected misstatement for every account tested during the engagement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 450.05 requires the auditor to establish and document the amount below which misstatements would be considered clearly trivial and need not be accumulated. This threshold is typically set well below performance materiality. Unlike documenting only overall materiality, the clearly trivial threshold specifically governs which individual misstatements the auditor tracks during fieldwork and represents a separate required documentation element.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Systemic vs. isolated control failures and severity classification
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Titan Energy Corp, the auditor tests 60 disbursement transactions and finds 2 lacking proper approval, both involving the same purchasing agent during a two-week period when the regular approver was on leave. In a separate test of 40 revenue transactions, the auditor finds 5 without proper credit approval, processed by different employees across the entire year. The auditor should classify:', '["The disbursement exceptions as less severe because they were isolated and situational, while the revenue exceptions suggest a more systemic problem", "Both findings at the same severity level because each represents a failure in the approval control", "The disbursement exceptions as more severe because they involve cash outflows with higher fraud risk", "Both findings as material weaknesses because any control exception creates a reasonable possibility of material misstatement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A10, the auditor evaluates control deficiency severity by considering the cause and frequency of exceptions. The disbursement exceptions are concentrated in one person during a known temporary period, suggesting an isolated event. The revenue exceptions span multiple employees and the full year, indicating a systemic breakdown. Unlike treating all exceptions equally, the auditor considers whether the root cause suggests the deficiency is pervasive or contained, which directly affects the likelihood dimension of severity.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Corrected misstatements still affecting risk assessment
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Hampton Consulting Group, the auditor identifies eight material misstatements in the revenue cycle, all of which management agrees to correct. After correction, the financial statements are fairly stated. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should:', '["Issue an unmodified opinion without further action since all misstatements were corrected", "Automatically classify the revenue controls as a material weakness due to the number of corrected errors", "Consider the implications of the corrected misstatements on the assessed risk of material misstatement and the audit strategy", "Require management to restate the interim financial statements issued during the period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.07, the auditor considers corrected misstatements and their implications, including whether they indicate risks of additional undetected misstatements. Eight material misstatements in one cycle suggest that assessed control risk may have been too low or substantive procedures insufficient. Unlike simply issuing an unmodified opinion, the auditor evaluates whether the nature and volume of corrected errors signal broader issues requiring additional procedures or risk assessment revision.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Magnitude and likelihood framework for deficiency severity
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Ironbridge Construction, the auditor identifies a control deficiency in the job cost allocation process. In evaluating the severity of this deficiency under AU-C 265, the auditor assesses two key dimensions. These dimensions are:', '["Frequency of the deficiency and the duration over which it has existed", "The magnitude and the likelihood of potential misstatement that could result from the deficiency", "The cost to remediate the deficiency and the time required for implementation", "The number of personnel involved and the dollar volume of transactions processed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, the severity of a control deficiency depends on (1) the magnitude of the potential misstatement resulting from the deficiency and (2) whether there is a reasonable possibility that controls will fail to prevent or detect a misstatement. These two dimensions form the framework for classifying deficiencies as significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. Unlike evaluating remediation cost, which is a management consideration, the auditor focuses on the risk characteristics of the deficiency.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L4 — Material weakness classification when no actual misstatement occurred
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Starfield Aerospace, the auditor discovers that the company has no control over manual journal entries posted to the general ledger — entries require no documentation, review, or approval. Despite extensive substantive testing, the auditor finds no misstatements attributable to manual journal entries. The auditor should:', '["Classify the absence of journal entry controls as a material weakness regardless of the substantive testing results", "Classify the deficiency as a significant deficiency since no actual misstatement resulted", "Not report the deficiency because the substantive procedures confirmed the financial statements are correct", "Defer classification until the subsequent year to determine whether misstatements eventually occur"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, a material weakness is determined by the reasonable possibility of material misstatement, not by whether an actual misstatement occurred. The complete absence of controls over manual journal entries represents a fundamental control gap with a reasonable possibility of undetected material misstatement. Unlike downgrading severity based on clean substantive results, the classification is based on the potential for misstatement, since substantive procedures cannot fully compensate for a complete absence of controls over journal entries.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Definition of "pervasive" effect of misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 705, when is the effect of a misstatement on the financial statements considered "pervasive"?', '["When the misstatement exceeds overall materiality by more than 50 percent", "When the misstatement is not confined to specific elements and represents a substantial proportion of the financial statements", "When management refuses to correct any identified misstatements regardless of amount", "When the misstatement affects only the income statement but not the balance sheet"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AU-C 705.05 defines pervasive effects as those not confined to specific elements, accounts, or items of the financial statements, or if confined, representing a substantial proportion, or being fundamental to users'' understanding of disclosures. Pervasiveness determines whether the auditor issues a qualified opinion (not pervasive) or an adverse opinion (pervasive). Unlike a fixed percentage threshold, pervasiveness is a qualitative assessment of how broadly the misstatement affects the financial statements.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
